<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Kentigern</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 614</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Saint Mungo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Kentigern, also called Mungo, was a 6th-century British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in the kingdom of Strathclyde and is the patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>. His 12th-century Life by Jocelin of Furness tells of his miracles and his dealings with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhydderch-hael">Rhydderch Hael</a>, though much of it is legend. His tomb lies in the crypt of Glasgow Cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-kentigern.jpg" alt="Saint Kentigern" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: dun_deagh · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Kentigern,_better_known_as_Mungo._Founder_and_patron_saint_of_Glasgow._(10062429595).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kentigern (Welsh Cyndeyrn) probably bore a British name meaning hound-lord, while Mungo is a pet name, possibly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a> for my dear one. According to the medieval accounts, his mother Teneu was a daughter of King Lleuddun, who ruled around what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a>, and his father was Owain mab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urien-rheged">Urien of Rheged</a>; the British Library manuscript says Teneu was raped, while other accounts tell of a love affair. Her furious father had her thrown from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traprain-law">Traprain Law</a>, and she drifted in a coracle across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> to Culross in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a>, where Mungo was born and raised by Saint Serf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the age of twenty-five, the Life relates, he began missionary work on the Clyde, building his church beside the Molendinar Burn where the medieval cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> now stands, and lived austerely there for some thirteen years. Driven out by an anti-Christian movement under a King Morken, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, staying with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-david">Saint David</a> and founding a church at Llanelwy (St Asaph), though S. Mundahl-Harris has shown his links with St Asaph to be a Norman invention. Invited back by the new king of Strathclyde, Riderch Hael, he set his seat at Hoddom and preached in Galloway; excavations at Hoddom have found a late 6th-century stone baptistery.</p><p>Mungo later returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, where a large community gathered around him, and at Kilmacolm he is said to have met Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> and exchanged pastoral staffs with him. Historians see little reason to doubt that he was one of the first evangelists of Strathclyde and probably the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Glasgow. Jocelin of Furness, who wrote his Life in about 1185, seems to have added episodes such as a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to promote the bishopric of Glasgow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In old age Mungo grew very feeble, and he is said to have died in his bath on Sunday 13 January; David McRoberts has argued that this garbles a collapse during a baptismal service. His shrine in Glasgow Cathedral was a great centre of pilgrimage until the Scottish Reformation. The four miracles of his Life, the bird, the tree, the bell and the fish, appear on the city's coat of arms, and its motto Let Glasgow flourish comes from his call. His feast is kept on 13 January.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Mungo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rhydderch Hael, a king of the late 6th century, appears in the lives of Columba and <b>Kentigern</b>, the patron saint of Glasgow.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-strathclyde">Kingdom of Strathclyde</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Culross, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.06,-3.63&amp;year=604" aria-label="Culross on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4471 255 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Saint Kentigern"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4921 525h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4921 525h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.06,-3.63&amp;year=604">What was here in 604 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
